  "answer": "Here is the detailed research evidence extracted directly from **\"1-Climate-change-impact-on-major-crop-yield-and-water-footprint-under-CMIP6-climate-projections.pdf\"**:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Core Models, Equations, and Calibration/Validation Metrics**\n\n* **Core Models**:\n  * **EPIC (Environmental Policy Integrated Climate) Model (v0810)**: Used to simulate crop growth, crop yield, and stress responses under changing atmospheric and climatic conditions [1].\n  * **CROPWAT 8.0 Model**: Used to calculate reference evapotranspiration (\\\\(ET_0\\\\)), crop evapotranspiration (\\\\(ET_c\\\\)), and effective rainfall (\\\\(P_{eff}\\\\)) to determine crop water footprints [2-4].\n\n* **Core Equations**:\n  1. **EPIC Crop Yield (\\\\(YLD\\\\))** [1]:\n     \\\\[YLD = HI \\cdot \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ WA \\cdot RA_i \\left(1 - \\exp(-0.65 \\cdot LAI_i)\\right) \\cdot 5000 \\right]\\\\]\n     *(where \\\\(HI\\\\) = harvest index, \\\\(WA\\\\) = biomass-energy ratio / radiation-use efficiency, \\\\(RA\\\\) = solar radiation, \\\\(LAI\\\\) = leaf area index)* [1].\n  2. **Biomass Regulation under Stress** [5]:\n     \\\\[\\Delta B = \\Delta B_p \\cdot REG\\\\]\n     *(where \\\\(\\Delta B_p\\\\) is potential biomass and \\\\(REG\\\\) is the minimum stress factor among water, temperature, nutrient, and aeration stress)* [5].\n  3. **Temperature Stress (\\\\(TS_i\\\\))** [6]:\n     \\\\[TS_i = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot \\frac{TG_i - Tb_j}{To_j - Tb_j}\\right)\\\\]\n     *(where \\\\(TG\\\\) is average soil surface temperature, \\\\(Tb\\\\) is base crop temperature, and \\\\(To\\\\) is optimal crop temperature)* [6].\n  4. **CROPWAT 8.0 Reference Evapotranspiration (\\\\(ET_0\\\\), FAO Penman–Monteith)** [3]:\n     \\\\[ET_0 = \\frac{0.408 \\Delta (R_n - G) + \\gamma \\frac{900}{T + 273} U_2 (e_s - e_a)}{\\Delta + \\gamma (1 + 0.34 U_2)}\\\\]\n  5. **Water Footprint (\\\\(WF_{total}\\\\)) Calculation** [2, 7, 8]:\n     \\\\[WF_{total} = WF_{green} + WF_{blue} + WF_{grey} = \\frac{CWU_{green}}{Y} + \\frac{CWU_{blue}}{Y} + WF_{grey}\\\\]\n     \\\\[WF_{grey} = \\frac{\\alpha \\cdot \\sum (N + ON) / (C_{max} - C_{nat})}{Y}\\\\]\n     *(where \\\\(\\alpha = 0.1\\\\) for nitrogen leaching/runoff, \\\\(C_{max} < 5\\text{ mg L}^{-1}\\\\) \\\\(\\text{NO}_3\\text{-N}\\\\), \\\\(C_{nat} = 0\\text{ kg m}^{-3}\\\\), and \\\\(Y\\\\) is crop yield in \\\\(\\text{ton ha}^{-1}\\\\))* [8].\n\n* **Calibration & Validation Performance Metrics**:\n  * *Note on PBIAS*: The study evaluated EPIC model performance using \\\\(R^2\\\\) (coefficient of determination, threshold \\\\(> 0.5\\\\)) and \\\\(NSE\\\\) (Nash–Sutcliffe efficiency, threshold \\\\(\\ge 0.60\\\\)) [9]; **PBIAS was not evaluated or reported** in this study.\n  * **Model Calibration (2013–2014 field data)** [10, 11]:\n    * **Rice** (\\\\(n=12\\\\)): Observed \\\\(5170 \\pm 241\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(5024 \\pm 225\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.73\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.67\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Maize** (\\\\(n=11\\\\)): Observed \\\\(3714 \\pm 544\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(3676 \\pm 588\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.67\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.65\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Soybean** (\\\\(n=10\\\\)): Observed \\\\(1444 \\pm 134\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(1451 \\pm 105\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.65\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.63\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Mung bean** (\\\\(n=10\\\\)): Observed \\\\(418 \\pm 77\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(425 \\pm 72\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.65\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.62\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Cassava** (\\\\(n=10\\\\)): Observed \\\\(13,250 \\pm 1399\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(13,465 \\pm 1083\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.64\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.63\\\\)** [11]\n  * **Model Validation (2021 field data)** [10, 11]:\n    * **Rice** (\\\\(n=18\\\\)): Observed \\\\(5722 \\pm 253\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(5872 \\pm 237\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.75\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.66\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Maize** (\\\\(n=15\\\\)): Observed \\\\(4378 \\pm 530\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(4418 \\pm 578\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.68\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.63\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Soybean** (\\\\(n=10\\\\)): Observed \\\\(1647 \\pm 152\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(1638 \\pm 135\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.66\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.62\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Mung bean** (\\\\(n=10\\\\)): Observed \\\\(491 \\pm 81\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(480 \\pm 88\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.64\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.61\\\\)** [11]\n    * **Cassava** (\\\\(n=12\\\\)): Observed \\\\(12,732 \\pm 1433\\\\) kg/ha | Simulated \\\\(12,500 \\pm 1248\\\\) kg/ha | **\\\\(R^2 = 0.63\\\\)**, **\\\\(NSE = 0.62\\\\)** [11]\n\n---\n\n### **2. Study Area Specifics**\n\n* **Location & Geomorphology**:\n  * **Province**: Phichit Province, Lower Northern Thailand [12].\n  * **Hydrology**: Yom and Nan rivers flow parallel through the province before merging into the Chao Phraya Basin [12].\n  * **Geomorphological Zones**:\n    1. **Alluvial Fan** (West side): Sand and gravelly sand; prone to **repeated drought** [12].\n    2. **Flood Plain** (Middle): Fine-grained clay and sandy clay; prone to **repeated floods** [12].\n    3. **Alluvial Plain** (East side): Gravel, sand, silt, and clay; prone to **repeated drought** [12].\n* **Districts, Sub-districts, and Topsoil Properties (0–10 cm)** [13]:\n  * **RRR** (Triple Rice, Irrigated): Sam Ngam District (Rang Nok Sub-district) — **Clay**, pH 5.3–5.6, Organic Matter (OM) 2.38–2.73% [13].\n  * **RR** (Double Rice, Rainfed): Taphan Hin District (Thap Man Sub-district) — **Sandy clay**, pH 5.1–5.2, OM 1.02–2.18% [13].\n  * **RM** (Rice–Maize): Wang Sai Phun District (Nong Phra Sub-district) — **Sandy clay**, pH 4.9–5.9, OM 1.55–2.05% [13].\n  * **RS** (Rice–Soybean): Pho Prathap Chang District (Phai Tha Pho Sub-district) — **Sandy clay**, pH 4.8–5.6, OM 2.01–2.15% [13].\n  * **RB** (Rice–Mungbean): Bueng Na Rang District (Pho Sai Ngam Sub-district) — **Silty clay**, pH 5.7–6.0, OM 0.69–1.35% [13].\n  * **MM** (Double Maize): Dong Charoen District (Samnak Kun Nen Sub-district) — **Silty clay**, pH 5.2–5.6, OM 2.24–2.34% [13].\n  * **CS** (Cassava): Thap Khlo District (Thap Khlo Sub-district) — **Sandy clay loam**, pH 5.7–5.9, OM 0.99–1.83% [13].\n* **Historical Baseline (1981–2014 Climate Data)** [14, 15]:\n  * Annual precipitation: **1648.4 mm/year** [15] (1983–2013 average: 1314.6 mm, wettest month = September at 321.3 mm) [16].\n  * Mean maximum temperature (\\\\(T_{max}\\\\)): **32.6 °C** (hottest month = April at 36.1 °C) [15, 16].\n  * Mean minimum temperature (\\\\(T_{min}\\\\)): **22.8 °C** (coldest month = January at 18.8 °C) [15, 16].\n\n---\n\n### **3. CMIP6 Climate Scenarios**\n\nThe study utilized an ensemble of **5 CMIP6 GCMs** (CESM2, MRI-ESM2-0, BCC-CSM2-MR, GFDL-ESM4, CanESM) downscaled and bias-corrected for Phichit [17].\n\n| Variable | Scenario | Baseline (1981–2014) | Near Future (2015–2039) | Mid Future (2040–2069) | Far Future (2070–2100) |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **Precipitation** *(mm/yr)* [15] | **SSP2-4.5** | 1648.4 | 1685.3 (+2.2%) | 1685.0 (+2.2%) | 1712.2 (+3.9%) |\n| | **SSP5-8.5** | 1648.4 | 1677.8 (+1.8%) | 1719.1 (+4.3%) | 1744.6 (+5.8%) |\n| **\\\\(T_{max}\\\\)** *(°C)* [15] | **SSP2-4.5** | 32.6 | 33.3 (+0.7 °C) | 34.2 (+1.5 °C) | 34.8 (+2.2 °C) |\n| | **SSP5-8.5** | 32.6 | 33.4 (+0.7 °C) | 34.8 (+2.1 °C) | 36.5 (+3.9 °C) |\n| **\\\\(T_{min}\\\\)** *(°C)* [15] | **SSP2-4.5** | 22.8 | 23.5 (+0.7 °C) | 24.3 (+1.5 °C) | 24.9 (+2.1 °C) |\n| | **SSP5-8.5** | 22.8 | 23.6 (+0.8 °C) | 24.9 (+2.1 °C) | 26.6 (+3.8 °C) |\n\n* *Seasonal extremes*: Peak \\\\(T_{max}\\\\) reaches **40.2 °C in April** by 2070–2100 under SSP5-8.5 [18]. High rainfall concentrates in September and October, while May receives lower rainfall than baseline [18].\n\n---\n\n### **4. Exact Yield Changes for Rice Cropping Systems**\n\n#### **A. Irrigated Area — Rapid Rice Rotation (RRR: Triple Rice)** [19]\n* **Baseline Yields**: 1st Rice = 6300 kg/ha | 2nd Rice = 5850 kg/ha | 3rd Rice = 6150 kg/ha [19]\n* **SSP2-4.5 Projections** [19]:\n  * **2015–2039**: 1st Rice = **6937 kg/ha** (+637 kg/ha; **+10.1%**) | 2nd Rice = **7000 kg/ha** (+1150 kg/ha; **+19.7%**) | 3rd Rice = **6585 kg/ha** (+435 kg/ha; **+7.1%**)\n  * **2040–2069**: 1st Rice = **7055 kg/ha** (+755 kg/ha; **+12.0%**) | 2nd Rice = **7395 kg/ha** (+1545 kg/ha; **+26.4%**) | 3rd Rice = **6909 kg/ha** (+759 kg/ha; **+12.3%**)\n  * **2070–2100**: 1st Rice = **7093 kg/ha** (+793 kg/ha; **+12.6%**) | 2nd Rice = **7780 kg/ha** (+1930 kg/ha; **+33.0%**) | 3rd Rice = **7097 kg/ha** (+947 kg/ha; **+15.4%**)\n* **SSP5-8.5 Projections** [19]:\n  * **2015–2039**: 1st Rice = **6327 kg/ha** (+27 kg/ha; **+0.4%**) | 2nd Rice = **5910 kg/ha** (+60 kg/ha; **+1.0%**) | 3rd Rice = **6214 kg/ha** (+64 kg/ha; **+1.0%**)\n  * **2040–2069**: 1st Rice = **5580 kg/ha** (-720 kg/ha; **-11.4%**) | 2nd Rice = **5330 kg/ha** (-520 kg/ha; **-8.9%**) | 3rd Rice = **5530 kg/ha** (-620 kg/ha; **-10.1%**)\n  * **2070–2100**: 1st Rice = **5264 kg/ha** (-1036 kg/ha; **-16.4%**) | 2nd Rice = **5112 kg/ha** (-738 kg/ha; **-12.6%**) | 3rd Rice = **5012 kg/ha** (-1138 kg/ha; **-18.5%**)\n\n#### **B. Rainfed Area — Double Rice (RR)** [19]\n* **Baseline Yields**: 1st Rice = 5930 kg/ha | 2nd Rice = 5850 kg/ha [19]\n* **SSP2-4.5 Projections** [19]:\n  * **2015–2039**: 1st Rice = **6106 kg/ha** (+176 kg/ha; **+3.0%**) | 2nd Rice = **6100 kg/ha** (+250 kg/ha; **+4.3%**)\n  * **2040–2069**: 1st Rice = **5975 kg/ha** (+45 kg/ha; **+0.8%**) | 2nd Rice = **5795 kg/ha** (-55 kg/ha; **-0.9%**)\n  * **2070–2100**: 1st Rice = **5804 kg/ha** (-127 kg/ha; **-2.1%**) | 2nd Rice = **5780 kg/ha** (-70 kg/ha; **-1.2%**)\n* **SSP5-8.5 Projections** [19]:\n  * **2015–2039**: 1st Rice = **5576 kg/ha** (-355 kg/ha; **-6.0%**) | 2nd Rice = **5417 kg/ha** (-433 kg/ha; **-7.4%**)\n  * **2040–2069**: 1st Rice = **5185 kg/ha** (-745 kg/ha; **-12.6%**) | 2nd Rice = **5130 kg/ha** (-720 kg/ha; **-12.3%**)\n  * **2070–2100**: 1st Rice = **5075 kg/ha** (-855 kg/ha; **-14.4%**) | 2nd Rice = **4812 kg/ha** (-1038 kg/ha; **-17.7%**)\n\n---\n\n### **5. Water Footprint (WF) Breakdown for Rice Seasons**\n\n* **Baseline Total WF for Rice**: **1206 to 1467 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)** [20].\n* **Irrigated Areas (RRR System Breakdown)** [20]:\n  * **1st Rice** (Rainy season): **Green WF dominates** (758–1318 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)); Blue WF is low (55.4–95 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)); Grey WF ranges from 223–349 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\) [20].\n  * **2nd Rice** (Dry season): Green WF = 287–371 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\); **Blue WF increases** (628–790 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)); Grey WF = 223–349 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\) [20].\n  * **3rd Rice** (Dry season): Green WF = 153–194 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\); **Blue WF highest** (803–1194 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)); Grey WF = 223–349 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\) [20].\n* **Rainfed Areas (RR System Breakdown)** [21]:\n  * **1st Rice**: Green WF dominated (~800–1400 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\) across scenarios) [21].\n  * **2nd Rice**: Blue WF dominated (~800–1200 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\) across scenarios) [21].\n  * **Grey WF**: **258 to 350 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)**, which is higher than in irrigated areas due to lower average rainfed yields [21].\n\n---\n\n### **6. Performance of Alternative Crops in Rainfed Adaptation Systems**\n\nNon-rice alternative crops consume **roughly half the water footprint** of rice production [22, 23].\n\n| Crop System & Crop | Baseline Yield (kg/ha) [24] | Future Yield Changes across Scenarios [24-27] | Baseline Total WF (\\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)) [20, 23] | Future Water Footprint Behavior [23, 28] |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **Mung bean** *(RB System)* | **480** | **+2.1% to +7.9%** (490–518 kg/ha) across ALL periods & scenarios [24, 25]. | **437** | **Lowest WF overall**. Average Green = 269, Blue = 88, Grey = 70 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\). WF decreases across all periods [23]. |\n| **Cassava** *(CS System)* | **12,500** | **+1.0% to +5.7%** (12,630–13,210 kg/ha) across ALL periods & scenarios [24, 27]. | **575** | Average Green = 178, Blue = 261, Grey = 104 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\). Total WF steadily declines to ~525–565 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\) [23]. |\n| **Maize** *(RM & MM Systems)* | RM: 4485 <br> MM1: 4485 <br> MM2: 4285 | **RM**: +0.2% to +1.2% in near/mid future; drops -1.3% to -1.6% in far future [25]. <br>**MM1**: **+0.5% to +15.8%** [26]. <br>**MM2**: +2.6% to +6.0% in near/mid future; drops -6.0% to -6.3% in far future [26, 29]. | **682 – 866** | WF decreases in near/mid future due to yield gains, but increases slightly in far future due to thermal stress [23, 28]. |\n| **Soybean** *(RS System)* | **1638** | **+0.2% to +1.5%** in near/mid future (1641–1663 kg/ha); drops -0.9% to -1.3% in far future [24, 25]. | **542** | WF decreases in near/mid future (~495–500 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)) and increases in far future (~595–620 \\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)) [23, 28]. |\n\n---\n\n### **7. Key Scientific Conclusions, Counter-Intuitive Facts, and Policy Recommendations**\n\n1. **Inverse Yield–Water Footprint Relationship (Core Scientific Principle)**:\n   * Water footprint per unit mass (\\\\(\\text{m}^3/\\text{ton}\\\\)) is **inversely proportional to crop yield** (\\\\(Y\\\\)) because \\\\(WF = \\frac{CWU}{Y}\\\\) [2, 30]. When yields increase under moderate CO2 fertilization and precipitation gains, the unit water footprint drops—and vice versa [30].\n2. **CO2 Fertilization Threshold Paradox**:\n   * Under moderate emissions (**SSP2-4.5**), increased rainfall and elevated CO2 concentration offset temperature increases, leading to yield gains in irrigated rice (+10.1% to +33.0%) [31, 32].\n   * Under extreme emissions (**SSP5-8.5**), excessive temperatures exceed optimal thresholds during flowering, triggering severe heat and water stress that overwhelms CO2 fertilization benefits, causing rice yields to collapse by up to **-18.5%** [31, 32].\n3. **Mung Bean & Cassava High Climate Resilience**:\n   * **Mung bean** thrives in the dry season because optimal temperatures (28–30 °C) fit dry-season conditions, and high dry-season temperatures suppress biotic stressors like Mungbean Yellow Mosaic Virus [29].\n   * **Cassava** exhibits exceptional drought tolerance and water-use efficiency, consistently increasing yields (+1.0% to +5.7%) across all scenarios [27, 33].\n4. **Policy Recommendations**:\n   * **Irrigated Areas**: Continue **Triple Rice Cropping (RRR)** under moderate warming scenarios, as guaranteed irrigation buffers heat stress [34, 35].\n   * **Rainfed Areas**: **Abolish double rice cropping (RR)** due to severe future yield losses (-6.0% to -17.7%) [31, 36]. Replace the 2nd rice crop with low water-consuming crops (**Mung bean, Maize, or Soybean**) [34-36].\n   * **Land-Use Conversion**: Transitioning rainfed lands to **Double Maize (MM)** or **Cassava (CS)** cuts total agricultural water consumption by **~50%** while maintaining agricultural stability against severe flood/drought cycles [22, 23, 35].\n\n---\n\n💡 *Next step: Would you like me to generate a comparative summary table or help you draft an adaptation policy brief based on these findings?*",
  "question": "For the document '1-Climate-change-impact-on-major-crop-yield-and-water-footprint-under-CMIP6-climate-projections.pdf': Extract detailed research evidence in structured format: 1. Core models, equations, calibration/validation metrics (R2, NSE, PBIAS) for EPIC and CROPWAT 8.0. 2. Study area specifics (Phichit districts, soil types, baseline 1981-2014). 3. Climate scenarios (SSP2-4.5, SSP5-8.5) across near (2015-2039), mid (2040-2069), and far (2070-2100) periods. 4. Exact yield changes (kg/ha and %) for Rapid Rice Rotation (RRR 1st, 2nd, 3rd crop) vs Rainfed (RR 1st, 2nd crop). 5. Water footprint breakdown (Green, Blue, Grey in m3/ton) for rice seasons. 6. Performance of alternative crops (mungbean, cassava, maize, soybean) in yield and water footprint. 7. Key scientific conclusions, counter-intuitive facts, and policy recommendations.",
