      "cited_text": "2.3. Crop yield simulation The EPICmodel version 0810was chosen to simulate crop yield. The EPIC model is an integrated model of the soil-water-atmosphere system (Williams et al., 1984; Williams et al., 1989; Sharpley and Wiliams, 1990), which is related to nitrogen and carbon cycling, surface runoff and nutrient leaching, crop growth, and yields (Williams et al., 2006; Marshall et al., 2013; Xiong et al., 2016). The EPIC model can evaluate the impacts of climate change on crop yield (Brown et al., 2000; Izaurralde et al., 2003; Tan and Shibasaki, 2003; Wang and Li, 2010; Arunrat et al., 2018; Arunrat et al., 2020; Arunrat et al., 2021b). In the EPIC model, the crop growth model uses radiation-use efficiency to calculate the photo-synthetic production of biomass. Crop yield (YLD) is calculated by the function of harvest index (HI), biomass-energy ratio (WA), solar radiation (RA), leaf area index (LAI), and growth period length as in Eq. (1) derived by Yin et al. (2014):"
    },
