      "cited_text": "yieldwill increase by 2.6–6.0% in the near andmid-future periods under both scenarios, except in the far-future period (decline by 6.0–6.3%; Table 5 and Fig. 4(f)). Cassava is likely to have a positive impact under future climate scenarios by increasing yield by 1.0–5.7% in all periods under both scenarios (Table 5 and Fig. 4(g)). Maize, soybean, mung bean, and cassava are generally grown in rain-fed conditions with high temperatures (>30 °C), low humidity, and low precipitation. These crops are considered drought tolerant due to surviving on limited soil moisture and soil water content. How-ever, drought stress and elevated temperatures are the main factors influencing yield changes. Nadeem et al. (2019) mentioned that a de-crease in soil moisture causes negative impacts on mung bean growth and productivity due to a reduction in photosynthesis and the harvest index, resulting in a decrease in plant dry weight (Thomas et al., 2004). In the study area, the simulation showed increasing mung bean yield in all periods under both scenarios (Table 5 and Fig. 4(e)) due to changes in future climate not exceeding the optimum values for mung bean growth. This was supported by Poehlman (1991), who mentioned that the optimum temperature range for mung bean growth is 28–30 °C, and that good conditions for seed germination are >15 °C of the average soil temperatures. However, higher temperatures >40 °C can reduce pol-len viability during the reproductive stage, causing a decline in yield (Kaur et al., 2015). Growing mung bean in the dry season can limit biotic stress, such as mung bean yellow mosaic disease (Begomovirus strains), due to high temperatures (Dharajiya et al., 2018). Therefore, mung bean is suitable for planting in the dry season in this area andwill be an effective adaptation strategy crop under future climate change."
